<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Ychsma</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1470 CE (approx.) · capital Pachacamac</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A coastal lordship of the Rímac and Lurín valleys centred on Pachacamac, the most famous oracle and pilgrimage site of the ancient Andes.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ychsma, or Ichma, emerged around 1100 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lurin-river">Lurín</a> and lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rimac-river">Rímac</a> valleys, where modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> stands, after the decline of Wari influence. It took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacamac">Pachacamac</a>, a sacred site occupied since the time of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lima-culture">Lima culture</a>, and made it the heart of its territory.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ychsma was a group of lordships united around the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacamac">Pachacamac</a>, the creator god whose oracle drew pilgrims from across the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/andecavi">Andes</a>. Its lords built a series of pyramids with ramps at Pachacamac and other sites such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armatambo">Armatambo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huaca-mateo-salado">Mateo Salado</a>. The economy relied on irrigation canals drawn from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rimac-river">Rímac</a> and on fishing.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> absorbed Ychsma peacefully around 1470 and respected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacamac">Pachacamac</a>, adding a Temple of the Sun and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acllahuasi">Acllahuasi</a>. In 1533 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-pizarro">Hernando Pizarro</a> looted the temple. Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> was founded in Ychsma territory in 1535, and Pachacamac is now a major archaeological site.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1100 CE – 1470 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South America</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.59%;width:7.36%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 CE – 1470 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pachacamac</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Cult of Pachacamac</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ichma, Señorío de Ychsma</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1285&amp;focus=ychsma" aria-label="Ychsma on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2568 2633 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ychsma lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2888 2889L3027 2937"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3027 2937h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2888 2889h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2888 2889h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Pachacamac. <a href="/atlas?year=1285&amp;focus=ychsma">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

